A||N||14||David Bartlett||Won't Start - Starter?||||1||
Z||000000||David Bartlett||10-08-2001||02:51 PM||||A little background.<BR>86, 2.8<BR>-when I turn the key the dash lights come on and the car won't start. It doesn't turn over nor is there any starter action. I can't really tell if the solenoid is clicking.<P>-I tested the battery and it reads 12.56volts and the little eye is green<P>-I replaced the altinator, 2 days ago because that was shot and wasn't charging the system.<P>What do you guys think?<P>I read about crossing the Battery terminal and Switch Terminal on the starter, Does this require me to have the ignition turned on? Should i remove any of the wires that connect to the starter?<P>Let me know... thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||David Bartlett||10-08-2001||02:51 PM||||I also checked the 20amp ignition/turn signal fuse.. it was ok.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||10-08-2001||04:43 PM||||Is it an auto or manual tranny?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||10-08-2001||05:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by David Bartlett:<BR><B>I read about crossing the Battery terminal and Switch Terminal on the starter, Does this require me to have the ignition turned on? Should i remove any of the wires that connect to the starter?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>This should be done with the tranny in neutral, the ignition off, and you don't need to remove wires. This will work with both auto and manual trannies.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||10-08-2001||08:38 PM||||Hey David!<BR>One thing I have found on several starters that fail in the manner you mention, is the copper strap that runs from the solenoid to the starter body breaks at the solenoid...<BR>I just get a longer screw and a piece of steel tubing cut to length, and bend the strap straight, drill a hole, and add the new screw and tube...<BR>Otherwise, I find the plastic cap on the solenoid broken...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000005||samoht||10-08-2001||08:44 PM||||i agree with Cliff, I would by pass neutral safety switch or clutch swicth...  Although you have good volage and the eye is green you could still have no amperage in the battery under load.. I would have the battery checked first since it is free.. and then try one for those switches.. maybe a bad ignition wire to the starter.. but I doubt it<P>85GT3.2||||reg||1
Z||000006||David Bartlett||10-09-2001||12:42 AM||||Update: In order of what I've done.<P>1)Checked volts between the Battery terminal on the starter and the switch terminal.. reads 12.5 volts<P>2) Pulled the starter out and with a 12.5v - 8amp battery charger tried to get it to run on the bench. No luck. This was done by placing the positive terminal to motor terminal and grounding to the case.<P>3) Un-hooked the solenoid and tried to get the motor to run by it self. No luck<P>4) Tried to get the solenoid to flip by its self.. no luck.<P>Does anyone know if the solenoid needs to be activated (ie the gear must be lowered) in order for the starter motor to run?<P>OH its a 4sp manual. And the copper strap seems to be fine. Its passing a current.||||reg||1
Z||000007||David Bartlett||10-09-2001||12:43 AM||||also the brushes on the motor look fine and don't look like there worn down.||||reg||1
Z||000008||David Bartlett||10-09-2001||12:45 AM||||One, One more thing. I tried crossing the terminals while it was on the car... I got an arc (generated by the current) but no Click from the solenoid or starter action.||||reg||1
Z||000009||kyote||10-09-2001||07:11 AM||||Change the solenoid..<BR>The current has to pass thru the solenoid then to the starter..<BR>besides.. a solenoid is cheaper than a starter..<BR>always go less expensive part first if you are unsure...<P>just my 2 cents...<P>------------------<BR>***************************<BR>'84 SE<BR>'84 SE (Strictly Parts Car)<BR>'86 SE||||reg||1
Z||000010||David Bartlett||10-09-2001||07:47 PM||||Alright this is getting to me.<P>-Had the battery load tested - Passed<BR>-Removed and had the Starter/solenoid tested - Passed<BR>-Replaced the Alternator 4 days ago<BR>-Lights and horn work fine.. ignition lights <BR>fine.<BR>-Volt meter tells me a reading of 12.6volts at the starter<P>I put all the parts back in and reinstalled the battery. Turned the key and heard the solenoid engage.. then it sounded like the starter got bog down. (maybe cause the teeth had to mesh right). I'm presently at a point where I started.. Turn the key and all the lights come on, etc but no action from anything else.<P>What should I try now?||||reg||1
Z||000011||David Bartlett||10-09-2001||08:57 PM||||By-passed the Clutch Switch... no luck<BR>Also jumped the starter manually again.. no luck<P><BR>Whats left?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierowrecker||10-09-2001||10:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by David Bartlett:<BR><B>Alright this is getting to me.<P>-Had the battery load tested - Passed<BR>-Removed and had the Starter/solenoid tested - Passed<BR>-Replaced the Alternator 4 days ago<BR>-Lights and horn work fine.. ignition lights <BR>fine.<BR>-Volt meter tells me a reading of 12.6volts at the starter<P>I put all the parts back in and reinstalled the battery. Turned the key and heard the solenoid engage.. then it sounded like the starter got bog down. (maybe cause the teeth had to mesh right). I'm presently at a point where I started.. Turn the key and all the lights come on, etc but no action from anything else.<P>What should I try now?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OOFTA Dave!<BR>One of two things, positive cable, or negative cable...<BR>With a volt meter, measure from the engine block to negative terminal while a friend tries to start the car, should be LESS than 1 volt...<BR>Same for the positive cable from solenoid to positive terminal...<BR>If the cables check ok, then it has to be the starter or solenoid...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000013||GarethWright||10-10-2001||02:40 AM||||When my starter was going, I could get under the car, and use a long ratchet extension and push the geared flywheel the starter turns a little, then the starter would start the car, my starter just couldnt push the engine anymore, and my new one works perfectly.<BR>I could see the geared wheel I was talking about through a gap where the engine meets the transmission on the drivers side of the car just in front of the rear wheel.  Theres holes in the wheel (balancing?) I used those to rotate the wheel a little.<BR>Good luck<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000014||David Bartlett||10-12-2001||02:08 PM||||Problem Solved:<P>It was a problem with the Starters mechanics, not the solenoid. Although the starter performed fine during the starter test it didn't catch the on again off again problem.<P>Thanks to everyone for the help and thanks to the friendly staff at Canadian Tire. Some people may trash that store but when it comes down to it there always there. Thanks store #122 Fairview.||||reg||1
